{
  "diagnosis": "The persistent kernel design, with a single program instance looping over all rows, resulted in a massive performance regression: latency increased from ~0.019 ms to ~0.83 ms (over 40x slower). This confirms severe underutilization: only one GPU thread block (program instance) processes the entire batch, leaving nearly all SMs idle. On this A100 GPU (108 SMs), the prior grid of 513 program instances ensured high parallel occupancy. The persistent approach is only reasonable for extremely small batches or when grid launch overhead dominates, which is not the case here. No correctness, masking, or shape errors were observed; the issue is purely due to loss of parallelism.",
  "outcome": "Failure. The persistent kernel structure is catastrophically slower for this problem size and hardware. This is a parallel decomposition failure, not an API or shape error.",
  "next_step": "Restore parallelism by mapping at least one program instance per row (grid=[M]), as in the previous fused kernel. If further tuning is desired, experiment with BLOCK size, num_warps, or hierarchical reduction for very wide N. Avoid persistent single-program designs except for extremely small M."
}

