{
  "diagnosis": "The persistent blockwise decomposition (grid=[2]) caused a severe performance regression, with median latency rising from ~0.017 ms (incumbent) to ~0.418 ms (over 24x slower). This is due to drastic underutilization of the GPU: only 2 program instances run, each looping over hundreds of rows, so nearly all SMs are idle for the entire kernel execution. While the kernel is functionally correct and covers all output with proper masking and reductions, it sacrifices the fine-grained parallelism needed to saturate the A100 GPU for this batch size (M=513). Launch overhead is negligible compared to the cost of serializing large chunks of the workload. This confirms that for moderate- or large-sized M, persistent kernel designs with few program instances are not competitive unless the workload is extremely small.",
  "outcome": "Failure. The kernel is correct but catastrophically slower due to poor parallel decomposition. The structure is not appropriate for this shape and device. No masking, coverage, or reduction bugs are present, but performance is unacceptable.",
  "next_step": "Revert to, or retain, the per-row parallel decomposition (grid=[M]), mapping one program instance per row. All reductions and normalization should occur in-register for each row, with proper masking for N < BLOCK. For this shape (N=781 < BLOCK=1024), further partitioning or persistent approaches are strictly suboptimal. Only consider persistent/M-chunking designs for extremely small M or host-bound launch regimes. For this and similar shapes, focus on tuning BLOCK, num_warps, or hierarchical reductions only for much larger N."
}

